-- reader-first.l4 with ROR in place of RAND. An ROR still runs its left side
-- (possession) over the whole trace before its right side (notice), so the
-- read at 400 does not see the notice the right side records at 10: what the
-- read saw depends on which side L4 runs first, not on when the notice was
-- given. Here the ROR's answer would come out the same either way, FULFILLED
-- by the notice at 10 with only the notice recorded (written `notice` ROR
-- `possession`, L4 never runs possession at all); it would not if possession
-- went on to the record its read allows, which is what an alternative does
-- if it is kept running once the other is fulfilled, as Hvitved's CSL
-- reduces a disjunction. What becomes of that alternative is not ruled
-- (NORM-LOG-SPEC N10's condition on lockstep), so L4 refuses the read rather
-- than answer from it: evaluation stops with an error naming the record and
-- both sides. (smucclaw/l4-ide#1069)

DECLARE Party IS ONE OF Landlord
DECLARE Act IS ONE OF `gives notice`, `seeks possession`

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`notice` MEANS
    PARTY Landlord
    MAY `gives notice`
    WITHIN 100
    HENCE COMMIT `notice given` IS TRUE HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`possession` MEANS
    PARTY Landlord
    MAY `seeks possession` PROVIDED (RECALL OFFICIAL's `notice given`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    WITHIN 1000
    HENCE COMMIT `possession sought` IS TRUE HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`the tenancy` MEANS `possession` ROR `notice`

#TRACE `the tenancy` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Landlord DOES `gives notice` AT 10
    PARTY Landlord DOES `seeks possession` AT 400
